  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1238 AM MDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nAn upper level high centered over the southwest part of the \ncountry will continue\u00a0to bring very warm to hot conditions to the \nregion this weekend and the first part of next\u00a0week. For today, \nthe airmass warms 2-3C at 700mb, with greater warming across the \nfar eastern plains. This will put high temperatures in the 95 to \n105 range across the urban corridor and eastern plains. Smoke \nseemed to play a slight role yesterday, knocking a couple degrees \noff our potential heating. The same will be possible today. The \n00Z models show more smoke for today than yesterday, but not a \nsignificant increase. Ingredients are there for Denver to reach \n100 degrees today, but the wildcard will be the smoke and how much\nit hinders warming. A slight speed max in the flow aloft will \ntravel across Wyoming today. Northern Colorado may see a slight \nincrease in winds this afternoon with gusts up to 25 mph. High-\nbased showers are expected to develop over the high country early \nthis afternoon and then progress eastward. Given how dry the \nairmass is and the very high cloud bases, very little, if any, \nrainfall will reach the ground. However, gusty outflow winds \nreaching up to 50 mph are expected from the showers.\u00a0Threat for \nshowers and gusty winds ends early this evening as the airmass \nstabilizes.\u00a0\n\nSunday's weather is looking very similar to Saturday's: hot, \nsmoky skies, and a chance for afternoon showers. One difference \nwill be an increase in moisture; precipitable water values will be\n0.6 to 0.8 (in), a few tenths higher than Saturday. This will \nincrease the number of showers and thunderstorms and also lead to \na better chance for rainfall to reach the ground. The best \ninstability will be over the higher terrain, thus the best chance \nfor showers and thunderstorms will be there.\u00a0\n\nMoisture continues to increase for Monday and Tuesday with \nprecipitable water values reaching 0.7 to 1.0 (in). These \nprecipitable water values are 100-125 percent of normal. The \nincrease in moisture and clouds should cool things slightly, but \nhighs are still expected to be in the mid to upper 90s across \nnortheast Colorado. Scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected to form over the high country on both days. They will \ncontinue to struggle to survive off the higher terrain due to the \nwarm air aloft from the upper level high and limited instability.\u00a0\n\nThe upper level high finally begins to weaken and shift southeast\nof the Rockies on Wednesday. Flow aloft turns south-southwest, \nhelping to pull monsoon moisture into the region. Precipitable \nwater values increase to 120-160 percent of normal. This is \nexpected to lead to scattered to numerous showers and \nthunderstorms. The loss of the upper level high and the increase \nin moisture will cause temperatures to cool with highs in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s across northeast Colorado. Moisture \ncontinues to increase for Thursday and Friday. Precipitable\u00a0water \nvalues climb to 140-180 percent of normal Thursday and Friday. \nThis will increase the chance of rainfall, and given the moist \nairmass, a threat of heavy rainfall and localized flooding could \nreturn. Temperatures cool off with highs only expected to reach \nthe mid 70s to lower 80s.",
